(1007854, 117999, 21124, '<td valign="top">Original picture:<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=120688" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/21124/thumb/120688.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/21124/thumb/120688.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/21124/thumb/120688.jpg"/></a></td>', 'Original picture:', '2004-11-12 13:09:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(1011899, 118058, 3087, '<td valign="top"><b>Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts</b> <br/>Nicely taken, nice angle, nice use of colour. Boring subject.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content/ Background</b> <br/>Composition is good. I like the low angle, and the creation of many diagonal lines through the frame. <br/>Restriction of colour to red, black and white works very well  good decision to reflect the limited red of the form with your background.<br/>I like the choice of shallow depth of field  it emphasises the length of the list and all the choices to be considered.<br/>My main negative on this image is that it''s an image that holds interest only within the context of the challenge. Whilst there''s nothing wrong with it there''s nothing about it that might make me want to come back and view it on it''s own merits.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>Exposure, focus seem good. Lighting nice and even. Interesting use of shallow depth of field.<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical</b> <br/>Nothing that stands out.<br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge</b> <br/>Yes.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo</b> <br/>By focusing so strongly on meeting the challenge I feel you''ve lost sight of the secondary goal (as I see it) which is to produce an image which is of interest outside of the challenge.<br/></td>', 'Critique Club  Initial thoughts Nicely taken, nice angle, nice use of colour. Boring subject. Composition/ Content/ Background Composition is good. I like the low angle, and the creation of many diagonal lines through the frame. Restriction of colour to red, black and white works very well  good decision to reflect the limited red of the form with your background.I like the choice of shallow depth of field  it emphasises the length of the list and all the choices to be considered.My main negative on this image is that it''s an image that holds interest only within the context of the challenge. Whilst there''s nothing wrong with it there''s nothing about it that might make me want to come back and view it on it''s own merits. Camera Work - Technical Exposure, focus seem good. Lighting nice and even. Interesting use of shallow depth of field. Digital Processing - Technical Nothing that stands out. Fits The Challenge Yes. My Opinion On The Photo By focusing so strongly on meeting the challenge I feel you''ve lost sight of the secondary goal (as I see it) which is to produce an image which is of interest outside of the challenge.', '2004-11-15 12:43:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(1012098, 117893, 3087, '<td valign="top"><b>Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts</b> <br/>Interesting and unusual idea. Room for technical improvement.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content</b> <br/>Content is a clever take on the challenge idea. There are all sorts of messages one can find: the message that struck me instantly when I first viewed this image during the voting period was that, for many of us, indecision is actually a sign of the luxury of choice and many people don''t have that choice. Freedom of choice is a powerful thing. That said, outside of the context of this challenge I''m not sure I''d realise that the theme was indecision.<br/>Visually, I like the repetition, particularly of the flag patches. I like the way that the soldier is off to one side and cropped closely to fill that side of the frame.<br/> <br/><b>Background</b> <br/>The brightness of that yellow background is unfortunately what keeps attracting my eye away from the actual content. <br/> <br/><b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>I think this image would benefit from deeper depth of field so that the soldier and the clothes in his closet were all in focus.<br/>I''d also suggest experimenting with lighting to improve and balance lighting on clothes, background and soldier''s skin.<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical</b> <br/>Nothing stands out.<br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge</b> <br/>Yes. <br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo</b> <br/>Interesting idea but the image itself isn''t hugely appealing on a visual level.<br/></td>', 'Critique Club  Initial thoughts Interesting and unusual idea. Room for technical improvement. Composition/ Content Content is a clever take on the challenge idea. There are all sorts of messages one can find: the message that struck me instantly when I first viewed this image during the voting period was that, for many of us, indecision is actually a sign of the luxury of choice and many people don''t have that choice. Freedom of choice is a powerful thing. That said, outside of the context of this challenge I''m not sure I''d realise that the theme was indecision.Visually, I like the repetition, particularly of the flag patches. I like the way that the soldier is off to one side and cropped closely to fill that side of the frame. Background The brightness of that yellow background is unfortunately what keeps attracting my eye away from the actual content.  Camera Work - Technical I think this image would benefit from deeper depth of field so that the soldier and the clothes in his closet were all in focus.I''d also suggest experimenting with lighting to improve and balance lighting on clothes, background and soldier''s skin. Digital Processing - Technical Nothing stands out. Fits The Challenge Yes.  My Opinion On The Photo Interesting idea but the image itself isn''t hugely appealing on a visual level.', '2004-11-15 15:34:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(1012298, 118099, 12694, '<td valign="top">i''m suprised nobody had a single negative thing to say about this image... it''s not bad, but here''s my problem with it:  why should anyone have to choose between beer and babe?  i always get them both.  so there, ha!</td>', 'i''m suprised nobody had a single negative thing to say about this image... it''s not bad, but here''s my problem with it:  why should anyone have to choose between beer and babe?  i always get them both.  so there, ha!', '2004-11-15 17:35:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(1012455, 118099, 25150, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by wickedpete:</b></div><hr/><i> i''m suprised nobody had a single negative thing to say about this image... it''s not bad, but here''s my problem with it:  why should anyone have to choose between beer and babe?  i always get them both.  so there, ha! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>usually one after the other!</td>', 'Originally posted by wickedpete: i''m suprised nobody had a single negative thing to say about this image... it''s not bad, but here''s my problem with it:  why should anyone have to choose between beer and babe?  i always get them both.  so there, ha!   usually one after the other!', '2004-11-15 19:00:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(1012496, 117966, 7543, '<td valign="top">*** Greetings from the Critique Club *** <br/> <br/>Hi Dawn, here are my thoughts on your Indecision entry:<br/> <br/><b>Challenge</b> <br/>The photo fits the challenge very nicely. I guess the whole challenge was built around the election and this pretty much sais what it''s all about. <br/> <br/>[/b]Composition[/b] <br/>I''d say the composition is o.k. but not much more than that. Putting more tention into the shot could emphasize the indecision part. Another angle and perspective could have given it more "juice", maybe shooting upward or something like that. <br/> <br/>[/b]Lighting[/b]<br/>Lighting is good, very even and no blown out parts (good for shooting car paint in the sun).<br/> <br/>[/b]Camera work[/b] <br/>Camera work is excellent, you have a very nice camera and put it to good use. Everything is sharp and in focus except maybe the background trees and you can tell that you used good gear the right way. Shooting this kind of subject suggest using deep depth of field and it works nicely for the shot. <br/> <br/>[/b]Post-Processing[/b] <br/>Post processing is very tasteful and the shot looks very "real", no over-sharpening, no burning or dodging or anything like that. Colors look very real and the overall look gives it a photojournalism feel, like you were documenting the scenery. I generally don''t like "complicated" borders (I''m always stuck with my own 10 pixel black one if I use one, lol) but yours is a good exsample of how it should be done  in this instance. The colors of the border also underline the challenge,  using "election colors", same as on the stickers. Good job. <br/> <br/>[/b]Title and Info[/b] <br/>Title is great and can be interperated in more than one way. The info is o.k. but I would want to see more, especially what lens used and at what focal length. Pasting full exif info (or the interesting parts at least) under your comments is easy and quick and informing for the rest of us. <br/> <br/>[/b]Overall (my opinion)[/b] <br/>In my opinion the photo lacks the so called wow-factor. Technically it''s very nicely done and it fits the challenge, and it even makes you think a bit, but visually it isn''t that rememberable. This challenge was very hard in my opinion though and I couldn''t find anything my self to shoot so I can''t blame you. Going for an unusual perspective could maybe have given it an extra point or so. <br/> <br/>Hopes this helps in some way, good luck in the future. (Excuse my english, I''m icelandic so my first language isn''t english) <br/> <br/>Best regards, Tyrkinn</td>', '*** Greetings from the Critique Club ***  Hi Dawn, here are my thoughts on your Indecision entry: Challenge The photo fits the challenge very nicely. I guess the whole challenge was built around the election and this pretty much sais what it''s all about.  [/b]Composition[/b] I''d say the composition is o.k. but not much more than that. Putting more tention into the shot could emphasize the indecision part. Another angle and perspective could have given it more "juice", maybe shooting upward or something like that.  [/b]Lighting[/b]Lighting is good, very even and no blown out parts (good for shooting car paint in the sun). [/b]Camera work[/b] Camera work is excellent, you have a very nice camera and put it to good use. Everything is sharp and in focus except maybe the background trees and you can tell that you used good gear the right way. Shooting this kind of subject suggest using deep depth of field and it works nicely for the shot.  [/b]Post-Processing[/b] Post processing is very tasteful and the shot looks very "real", no over-sharpening, no burning or dodging or anything like that. Colors look very real and the overall look gives it a photojournalism feel, like you were documenting the scenery. I generally don''t like "complicated" borders (I''m always stuck with my own 10 pixel black one if I use one, lol) but yours is a good exsample of how it should be done  in this instance. The colors of the border also underline the challenge,  using "election colors", same as on the stickers. Good job.  [/b]Title and Info[/b] Title is great and can be interperated in more than one way. The info is o.k. but I would want to see more, especially what lens used and at what focal length. Pasting full exif info (or the interesting parts at least) under your comments is easy and quick and informing for the rest of us.  [/b]Overall (my opinion)[/b] In my opinion the photo lacks the so called wow-factor. Technically it''s very nicely done and it fits the challenge, and it even makes you think a bit, but visually it isn''t that rememberable. This challenge was very hard in my opinion though and I couldn''t find anything my self to shoot so I can''t blame you. Going for an unusual perspective could maybe have given it an extra point or so.  Hopes this helps in some way, good luck in the future. (Excuse my english, I''m icelandic so my first language isn''t english)  Best regards, Tyrkinn', '2004-11-15 19:18:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(1012532, 118130, 7543, '<td valign="top">*** Greetings from the Critique Club *** <br/> <br/>Hi Cole, here are my thoughts on your Indecision entry:<br/> <br/><b>Challenge</b> <br/>The entry fits the challenge very nicely, don''t think anyone can disagree with me on that one. <br/> <br/>[/b]Composition[/b] <br/>Your composition is very nice, good use of the square format and your head and the coin are placed nicely within the frame (must have been a pain in the rear to get the coin in the right place with a timer!)<br/> <br/>[/b]Lighting[/b]<br/>Lighting is great, good use of an external flash with good detail on shirt and skin and it doesn''t have that "hard-flash-look" that I don''t like. The cap might have been slightly lighter to seperate it from the background. <br/> <br/>[/b]Camera work[/b] <br/>Great work here, everything within the dof, very sharp and clear in all ways. You know your gear. <br/> <br/>[/b]Post-Processing[/b] <br/>Great b/w, very clean and simple, nothing looking "fixed" or faked. <br/> <br/>[/b]Title and Info[/b] <br/>Title is good (and true) and the info, although short, sais everything it needs to say imho. <br/> <br/>[/b]Overall (my opinion)[/b] <br/>Good shot in most ways but might be better. Lacks the so called wow-factor, but then again this challenge and the wow factor is hard to combine (couldn''t find anything to shoot my self). <br/> <br/>Hope this helps (excuse my english, i''m icelandic)<br/> <br/>Best regards, Tyrkinn</td>', '*** Greetings from the Critique Club ***  Hi Cole, here are my thoughts on your Indecision entry: Challenge The entry fits the challenge very nicely, don''t think anyone can disagree with me on that one.  [/b]Composition[/b] Your composition is very nice, good use of the square format and your head and the coin are placed nicely within the frame (must have been a pain in the rear to get the coin in the right place with a timer!) [/b]Lighting[/b]Lighting is great, good use of an external flash with good detail on shirt and skin and it doesn''t have that "hard-flash-look" that I don''t like. The cap might have been slightly lighter to seperate it from the background.  [/b]Camera work[/b] Great work here, everything within the dof, very sharp and clear in all ways. You know your gear.  [/b]Post-Processing[/b] Great b/w, very clean and simple, nothing looking "fixed" or faked.  [/b]Title and Info[/b] Title is good (and true) and the info, although short, sais everything it needs to say imho.  [/b]Overall (my opinion)[/b] Good shot in most ways but might be better. Lacks the so called wow-factor, but then again this challenge and the wow factor is hard to combine (couldn''t find anything to shoot my self).  Hope this helps (excuse my english, i''m icelandic) Best regards, Tyrkinn', '2004-11-15 19:31:45.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(1013927, 118108, 21862, '<td valign="top">Wow, great capture, Matt!!!  Really excellent!  I LOVE that you''ve captured the coin as a still object, though perhaps a twist in the dimension of its travel might be more convincing of its motion.  Not sure how you set that one up, though.  The high contrast is wonderful, and really works with the subject!</td>', 'Wow, great capture, Matt!!!  Really excellent!  I LOVE that you''ve captured the coin as a still object, though perhaps a twist in the dimension of its travel might be more convincing of its motion.  Not sure how you set that one up, though.  The high contrast is wonderful, and really works with the subject!', '2004-11-16 16:32:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(1031082, 117830, 656, '<td valign="top">Great initial impact - the dog, the eyes.  The shallow DoF and fall-off to the background also add to this effect of being ''in your face'' Very effective.  However, the left side of the shot just doesn''t work for me, with the square and pencil - they lead my eye out of the scene - particularly the bright yellow and reds in the pencil and the line shape they form pointing away from the subject and off the page to the left.  <br/> <br/>This can be helped a whole lot by cropping the shot square, around the dog.  The right hand two thirds of the image are strong enough and with a centered crop the dog is the real focus of the shot.<br/> <br/>Now following convention, you should use rule of thirds, right ?  Well the reason is that if you have a subject bang in the center of the frame it takes all the attention, is very arresting, static and generally dominates the composition.  In this case - that is what is happening anyway.  If you compose it in a square frame by cropping the left third - you''ll get an even stronger, more arresting scene.  The elements in it build to that effect, the composition will enhance that and I think it''ll be stronger all around.  <br/> <br/>After doing that, you are still left with the piece of wood jutting in from the left - which does still clutter it a bit, but I think this crop would make for a much stronger image - try it and see how you like it!  Then maybe try and re-create this sort of image, with just the green grass background and without the other elements - try the off center and very centered/ square compositions with those as well and see which you prefer.</td>', 'Great initial impact - the dog, the eyes.  The shallow DoF and fall-off to the background also add to this effect of being ''in your face'' Very effective.  However, the left side of the shot just doesn''t work for me, with the square and pencil - they lead my eye out of the scene - particularly the bright yellow and reds in the pencil and the line shape they form pointing away from the subject and off the page to the left.   This can be helped a whole lot by cropping the shot square, around the dog.  The right hand two thirds of the image are strong enough and with a centered crop the dog is the real focus of the shot. Now following convention, you should use rule of thirds, right ?  Well the reason is that if you have a subject bang in the center of the frame it takes all the attention, is very arresting, static and generally dominates the composition.  In this case - that is what is happening anyway.  If you compose it in a square frame by cropping the left third - you''ll get an even stronger, more arresting scene.  The elements in it build to that effect, the composition will enhance that and I think it''ll be stronger all around.   After doing that, you are still left with the piece of wood jutting in from the left - which does still clutter it a bit, but I think this crop would make for a much stronger image - try it and see how you like it!  Then maybe try and re-create this sort of image, with just the green grass background and without the other elements - try the off center and very centered/ square compositions with those as well and see which you prefer.', '2004-11-24 10:18:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(1044951, 118104, 3575, '<td valign="top">What a wonderful shot Sonja. Another emotive image. I''m one of the ones who didn''t get it. I''m so dense I was looking for butterflies on Lila Mae''s costume trying to find a connection to the title! Good thing I didn''t vote :) I Love it Love it Love it!</td>', 'What a wonderful shot Sonja. Another emotive image. I''m one of the ones who didn''t get it. I''m so dense I was looking for butterflies on Lila Mae''s costume trying to find a connection to the title! Good thing I didn''t vote :) I Love it Love it Love it!', '2004-12-03 16:13:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(1077342, 118155, 22734, '<td valign="top">This is a very good composition. The use of the two candidates, the patriotic colors across the forehead with the flag restraining makes for a superb image. There is talent here and continue to apply your imagination in these presentations and you are certain to throw a couple out of the park.</td>', 'This is a very good composition. The use of the two candidates, the patriotic colors across the forehead with the flag restraining makes for a superb image. There is talent here and continue to apply your imagination in these presentations and you are certain to throw a couple out of the park.', '2004-12-25 22:12:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078387, 117936, 26050, '<td valign="top">i love it much. :))))</td>', 'i love it much. :))))', '2004-12-27 01:15:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(1150207, 117917, 23458, '<td valign="top">Well I''m amazed you managed to coax a macro of this quality out of the good old A70 - my girlfriend has one and it''s certainly a capable camera (more versatile than my old olympus p&amp;s for sure) but still, I had no idea you could do this with it!<br/> <br/>So well done, I really like this a lot.</td>', 'Well I''m amazed you managed to coax a macro of this quality out of the good old A70 - my girlfriend has one and it''s certainly a capable camera (more versatile than my old olympus p&s for sure) but still, I had no idea you could do this with it! So well done, I really like this a lot.', '2005-02-04 06:15:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(1150221, 117917, 8759, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by samtrundle:</b></div><hr/><i> Well I''m amazed you managed to coax a macro of this quality out of the good old A70 - my girlfriend has one and it''s certainly a capable camera (more versatile than my old olympus p&amp;s for sure) but still, I had no idea you could do this with it!<br/> <br/>So well done, I really like this a lot. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Thanks a lot.<br/>I used <a href="//www.pbase.com/gallery?gallery_id=780350" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">this</a>and<br/> a lot of patience. I took around 30 shots to be happy with it.<br/>Have a look at <a href="//www.shutterstock.com/pic.mhtml?&amp;id=131562&amp;searchterm=&amp;searchtermx=&amp;anyorall=&amp;attr=&amp;value=" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">this</a> <br/>This was different. I used the same box but I attached my old Zenit lens es (inverted)<br/></td>', 'Originally posted by samtrundle: Well I''m amazed you managed to coax a macro of this quality out of the good old A70 - my girlfriend has one and it''s certainly a capable camera (more versatile than my old olympus p&s for sure) but still, I had no idea you could do this with it! So well done, I really like this a lot.  Thanks a lot.I used thisand a lot of patience. I took around 30 shots to be happy with it.Have a look at this This was different. I used the same box but I attached my old Zenit lens es (inverted)', '2005-02-04 06:35:19.000000', NULL, 0),
